<BaseLayout title="Reliable AI Systems — Jonas El Gammal" description="Graphsmith: typed graph planning, deterministic validation, repair, and execution.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Research · AI systems</span><h1>Make structure deterministic.</h1><p>Graphsmith explores how language models can plan executable programs without also being responsible for their structural correctness.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose"><figure class="figure"><img src="/assets/img/graphsmith_pipeline.svg" alt="Graphsmith planning, compilation, repair, and execution pipeline"/></figure><h2>Semantic planning, compiled</h2><p>Language models often produce workflows that look plausible but contain missing bindings, invalid control flow, or invented capabilities. Graphsmith lets the model propose intent in a typed intermediate representation; deterministic machinery compiles, validates, repairs, executes, and traces the result.</p><h2>Current questions</h2><ul><li>Can graph representations make generated programs easier to inspect and repair?</li><li>When should a local region be regenerated instead of rebuilding the whole plan?</li><li>Can successful subgraphs become reusable skills with explicit contracts and provenance?</li><li>How should these systems be evaluated beyond narrow task success?</li></ul><p>This is an experimental AI-systems project, not yet a general-purpose programming environment. <a href="https://github.com/jonaselgammal/Graphsmith">Explore the source code</a>.</p></article>
</BaseLayout>
